<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>More modeling</title>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        If you were to jump from an airplane with a parachute, gravity
        will cause your 
        downward velocity to increase at the rate of <m>g \approx 9.8</m>
        meters per second squared. At the same time, wind resistance causes
        your velocity to decrease at a rate proportional to the velocity. 
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using <m>k</m> for the constant of proportionality, write a
          differential equation that describes the rate of change of
          the downward velocity <m>v</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="37">
        <p>
          <md>
            \frac{dv}{dt} = 9.8 - kv
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find any equilibrium solutions and decide whether they are
          stable or unstable. Your result should depend on <m>k</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="37">
        <p>
          There is a stable equilibrium at <m>v = \frac{9.8}k</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose your initial velocity is <m>v(0)=0</m>.  Find the
          velocity as a function of time.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="37">
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \frac1{9.8-kv}\frac{dv}{dt} \amp = 1
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \int\frac1{9.8-kv}~dv \amp = \int 1~dt
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              -\frac1k\ln|9.8-kv| \amp = t+C
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              v \amp = Ae^{-kt} + \frac{9.8}{k}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              v \amp = \frac{9.8}{k}\left(1-e^{-kt}\right)
            </mrow>
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If you find that your velocity stabilizes at 54 meters per
          second, is the value of <m>k</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="37">
        <p>
          <m>\frac{9.8}k = 54</m> so
          <md>k=\frac{9.8}{54}\approx0.181</md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          How long does it take to reach 50% of the terminal velocity?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="37">
        <p>
          Solving <m>v(t)=\frac12 54 = 27</m> gives
          <m>e^{-kt}=\frac12</m> so that
          <md>
            t = \frac{\ln\left|\frac12\right|}{-k} = 3.8
          </md>
          seconds.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The logistic equation is sometimes used to model the spread of
        a disease through a population.  We will call <m>p(t)</m> the
        fraction of people who have had the disease and suppose
        that
        <md>
          \frac{dp}{dt} = 0.2p(1-p)
        </md>.
        Suppose that initially one tenth of the population has had
        the disease so that <m>p(0)=0.1</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What happens to <m>p(t)</m> after a very long time?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="37">
        <p>
          <m>p=1</m> is a stable equilibrium so eventually everyone
          has had the disease.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find a formula for <m>p(t)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="37">
        <p>
          Using our earlier expression for logistic growth, we have
          <md>
            p(t)=\frac{1}{1+9e^{-0.2t}}
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          At what time is <m>p</m> changing most rapidly?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="37">
        <p>
          With logistic growth, the greatest rate of change occurs
          when <m>p</m> is halfway between 0 and the carrying
          capacity, which is 1 in this case.  So when <m>p(t)=0.5</m>.
          This leads to
          <md>
            <mrow>
              p(t) = \frac1{1+9e^{-0.2t}} \amp = 1/2
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              1+9e^{-0.2t} \amp = 2
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              t \amp = \frac{\ln(1/9)}{-0.2} \approx 11.0
            </mrow>
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          How long until 80% has had the disease?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="37">
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>
              p(t) = \frac1{1+9e^{-0.2t}} \amp = 0.8
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              1+9e^{-0.2t} \amp = \frac54
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              t \amp = \frac{\ln(1/36)}{-0.2} \approx 16.3
            </mrow>
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
